Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  this group of fathers have gathered  before to talk  so what's up fellas how you guys doing  doing all right man good job  yeah man thanks for coming but this time  they're taking their message public  hoping this video will spark real change  we came together today just to have a  quick conversation about  some of our feelings about about growing  up black and having our kids grow up  black  in east york and being part of the  education system  for the six men this is a place to share  experiences  and express their concerns for viewers  it's a place to listen  to learn and to understand  it was here in this park in east toronto  the dads met  and began talking over the years these  passing conversations grew into so much  more  michael avis and carrie cox are two of  the fathers in the group  we'll talk about race we'll talk about  what it's like working in the office  where you're the only one  we'll talk about all those things and  that is a bond that  with you know some of my friends who  aren't black they just don't get it  it's not their fault but they just don't  understand that kind of that  conversation  talk about politics we talk about you  know  you know being black in 2021 2021  black lives matter push for diversity  and equity and  workplace and there's some cynicism  about it some hope to do with it  what it means for our kids what it means  for us we just get into all kinds of  philosophical discussions about that  kind of stuff what originally started  out  as chance encounters has now turned into  a deep  bond a friendship each dad can depend on  when they need support you guys are  getting together  you have discourse you're talking about  what's going on and all of a sudden  covet hits and the world shuts down tell  us what  you learned from your kids learning  online during the pandemic  there was one instance where i was  observing the class and i was hearing  some things that kind of made me a  little bit uncomfortable  and the first thing i did was i sent a  whatsapp to these guys i said hey fellas  i heard this  you know what should i do and  immediately they said you need to call  it out  what were these teachers teaching that  uh kind of got your back up a little bit  first i want to preface  that i'm a teacher the teachers have my  full support  that being said i don't think that  there's proper  training for teachers to talk about race  i don't think there's proper training  for them to be able to speak  meaningfully  this poor teacher is being asked to talk  about  this and she doesn't have the training  and the understanding of how her words  can affect  her students and i i felt badly for her  but i also felt badly for  my kids when i thought what are they  thinking right now  that wasn't the only concern they had  part of this discussion that we're  having today is hoping so in march  they recorded their thoughts and emailed  the video to their kids principals and  vice principals  what were you hoping to accomplish by  putting the video out there  there's definitely going to be some  teachers out there maybe some principals  out there who are going to  appreciate that it will give them a  perspective that maybe  they hadn't thought about and it might  influence  the way they approach this next time  there's a huge disconnect in the kinds  of language  in the kinds of knowledge that educators  are having particularly white educators  but other racialized educators are  having about blackness and blackness in  canada  funke is an assistant professor in the  department of history  at the university of toronto there are  people in our school boards  there are people who are working in the  ministry of education  who are deeply invested in creating  models and creating  important language that educators need  to use and adapt  and take into the classroom but for some  reason there is this disconnect  that's because there isn't a standard  level of training for canadian teachers  that differs from province to province  so there are pockets and schools and  institutions that are doing it well  there are educators in individual  classrooms that are thinking critically  about how to address and speak to their  black students and then there are  schools that aren't doing it well and as  a result we don't have a uniform way of  understanding whether or not we're doing  it right  what i see in the work that i'm doing  now is that we seem to have taken a  major step  backwards because we can't seem to deal  with a multiplicity of issues  sadie keane has been fighting for equity  in schools for more than 50 years  she was vancouver school board's first  black trustee  and helped to write the race relations  policy in the 1980s  in terms of policy it is really easy  for institutions right now  to say oh well we've done that  that's been dealt with it's often people  who have not  been directly impacted or affected by  racism and hate who are making those  decisions  experts say canada's inability to move  the needle forward  is caused by the country's failure to  confront its long-standing history  with colonialism when colonial models  came in  education was structured around  religious based institutions  and then it starts to consolidate itself  and when it does that  it doesn't envision certain groups of  people as being part of the canadian  citizenry  and as a result racialized folks  indigenous folks  black people are excluded from those  conversations entirely  many canadians assume segregated schools  are unique to the us  but it happened in canada too one  example  ontario's common school act of 1850 led  to segregated schools  more often than not the common school  act was actually used  to separate children from classroom  spaces to  further demarcate that separation by  race  and this becomes a continuous problem  that we see even in our own  current present day schooling practices  nova scotia had legislation as well  and to some extent similar practices  were taking place in prince edward  island  new brunswick and british columbia i do  think that we need to have a complete  overhaul of our education system or  really have  a forceful conversation about  educational reform but black community  members have been talking about this  for as far as 1960s and 1970s  when they've been asking right our  curriculums to change to speak to the  black experience in  really important ways and we've not yet  been able to do that well  we need to reimagine education  there's so much that we can do right  that the opportunities for  making and supporting education to truly  be much more inclusive and dynamic  and really support them to be able to  move into a world that  is calling on all of us to be  our very best selves  it's why the dads made the video to help  provide  schools the tools to reimagine back at  the park the friends have come  together again what can you suggest they  do to change or to improve there's a lot  of questions about what the  responsibility of a black parent  or a black or brown person is in  educating people about what they should  think about and how they should speak to  people  we took it as let's give some  specific tools that educators can do to  start to make  immediate change i'd just love to see  more  you know educators that look like me  whether it's supply teachers or  for plays or musical presentations or  scientists and so on to share michael  points to the fact that the fathers are  available to help  he's a college professor carrie is an  engineer  and the others well there's an executive  a doctor  a photographer a project manager and one  has a book being released later this  year  so principals have a deep well of  resources to draw upon  be mindful of the language othering  language is damaging  using words like they or them when  referring to  different cultures and different ethnic  backgrounds  automatically excludes so we should be  talking about us  as a community and not about well they  need this and black lives matter  them it's not them it's their part of  our community they're part of  us inclusive language inclusive  literature  and representation those are three  things that we said to these principals  these are things that you can do this is  low-hanging fruit  the fathers emailed the four schools  their kids attend  at the time of our interview only one  school had acknowledged receiving the  video  and the fathers offered to help global  news contacted the schools  since then all principals have reached  out to the respective dads  where do you take this from here i don't  know  we're at a watershed moment i think  there's a lot of  awareness there's a lot of every school  has a diversity committee  it's on the radar people are aware of it  i just hope and this is the reason that  i'm here  these are not just words the fact that  you put a committee somewhere does not  mean anything if that committee doesn't  do anything  we've given this to you on a silver  platter we've said here are some easy  things that you can do to make change  i don't know what the next step is but i  hope this can start a conversation a  deeper conversation about what are we  really doing are we ticking boxes or are  we really doing something different  and that i don't have the answer to

